// fuzz-mutate-06: affected_stmts tracks only `let x =` binds; a statement
// reading a name of a destructuring let built from the value is filed MISPLACED
// command: graphix-fuzz typemorph design/review-2026-10-05/repro/fuzz-mutate-06.gx
// (or the program alone on stdin: graphix-fuzz typemorph-one <outfile>)
// expected: no flips. Both mutants are refused, rightly, at `p + 1`
// (`graphix --check` on either mutant: in `let z = p + 1`), where `w`'s new
// type reaches through `let (p, q) = (w, 2)`, a let built from `w`.
// observed: exit 1,
// TYPEFLIP ... widen-consumer#1: MISPLACED: N: cannot compute '_N: [iN, string] + iN ...
//   mutant: { let w = select 1 == 1 { true => 1, false => "tm" }; let (p, q) = (w, 2); ... }
// TYPEFLIP ... retype#12: MISPLACED: N: cannot compute string + iN ...
//   mutant: { let w = "tm"; let (p, q) = (w, 2); ... }
// The right sites are statements 0, 1 and 3 (mustreject.rs:800): `p` is never
// added to `affected`. With `let p = (w, 2).0` instead: REJECT ok for both.
{ let w = 1; let (p, q) = (w, 2); let z = p + 1; let y = w + 3; (z, y, q) }
